findings.body:
Before, a missing DB row (`conversation variable not found in the database`) or a DB error made the assigner node fail, so the user saw an error. Now the same failure is raised inside `on_event`, and `EventManager._notify_layers` catches every layer exception and only logs it:

::code{file="api/core/workflow/graph_engine/event_management/event_manager.py" lines="173-186"}

The layer itself also returns silently when `conversation_id is None`. The old v1 node raised `conversation_id not found`, and v2 raised `ConversationIDNotFoundError` outside the debugger:

::diff{file="api/core/app/layers/conversation_variable_persist_layer.py" lines="34-36"}

The net effect is that the chat turn succeeds while the conversation variable silently doesn't persist, and the next turn sees the old value. There's also no per-item isolation: if one variable raises in the loop, the remaining variables in the same event aren't persisted.

If failing silently is the intended trade-off, put a try/except around each item that logs which selector and conversation failed, and say so in the PR. Otherwise, surface the failure, for example by aborting via the command channel or failing the run in `on_graph_end`.

The `ConversationVariableUpdater` protocol allows batching implementations where `flush` writes the buffered updates. Calling `flush()` after every `update()` defeats that. The old v2 node called `flush()` once after its loop.

::diff{file="api/core/app/layers/conversation_variable_persist_layer.py" lines="52-53"}

Move `flush()` out of the loop and call it only if at least one update happened. The current impl's `flush` is a no-op, so this doesn't change behavior today.

The new constructor only forwards its arguments to `super().__init__`. It adds a `TYPE_CHECKING` import block and nothing else:

::diff{file="api/core/workflow/nodes/variable_assigner/v2/node.py" lines="56-68"}

It can be deleted along with the `TYPE_CHECKING` imports. Separately, the docstring of `core/workflow/conversation_variable_updater.py` still says the protocol is for use by the assigner nodes, and that each node should create its own instance. Neither is true anymore.
